CAT = "cmaLegal"; SEG = "LEG"
Q = [
("Which law strengthened HIPAA enforcement and added breach notification rules as practices moved to electronic records?",
 "The HITECH Act", ["The ADA Amendments Act", "The Controlled Substances Act", "CLIA"],
 "The HITECH Act (2009) promoted electronic health records, raised HIPAA penalties and requires notice to patients when unsecured PHI is breached."),
("A patient asks for a copy of her medical record. Under HIPAA, what must the practice generally do?",
 "Provide it within 30 days", ["Refuse unless a lawyer asks", "Send it only to her insurer", "Let her view it only on site"],
 "HIPAA gives patients a right to access and get copies of their records, generally within 30 days, with one possible 30-day extension. A reasonable cost-based fee may apply."),
("A patient believes an entry in her record is wrong. What right does HIPAA give her?",
 "To request an amendment", ["To delete the entry herself", "To sue before any review", "To have the record destroyed"],
 "Patients may ask the practice to amend their record. If the practice declines, the patient can add a statement of disagreement to the record."),
("A substance use disorder treatment program receives a request for a patient's records. What do federal confidentiality rules require?",
 "Specific written consent", ["Only a verbal OK from the patient", "A phone call from any provider", "No consent if insurance asks"],
 "Records of federally assisted substance use disorder programs have extra protection under 42 CFR Part 2 and need specific written consent for most releases."),
("What does the Genetic Information Nondiscrimination Act (GINA) prohibit?",
 "Using genetic data to deny coverage", ["Ordering any genetic testing", "Storing genetic results in the chart", "Telling patients their results"],
 "GINA bars health insurers and employers from using genetic information to decide coverage, premiums, hiring or firing. It does not ban testing."),
("A patient who signed a release of information wants to cancel it. What is required?",
 "A written revocation", ["A phone call to the office", "Nothing; it expires at once", "A court order"],
 "An authorization is revoked in writing. The revocation stops future releases but cannot undo disclosures already made under it."),
("How should old paper records containing PHI be disposed of?",
 "Shredded or incinerated", ["Placed in office recycling", "Thrown in regular trash", "Stored in an unlocked bin"],
 "PHI must be made unreadable before disposal, for example by shredding, pulping or burning. Recycling bins and trash expose patient information."),
("A 17-year-old who is married and lives on her own seeks treatment. Which type of minor can usually consent to her own care?",
 "An emancipated minor", ["Any minor over 12", "A minor with a parent's card", "No minor in any situation"],
 "Emancipated minors, such as those who are married or in the military, are treated as adults for consent. Exact rules for minors vary by state."),
("An unconscious patient is brought in and needs emergency care. How is consent handled?",
 "Implied consent applies", ["Care waits for written consent", "A neighbor must sign first", "Only first aid may be given"],
 "When a delay would endanger life and the patient cannot consent, the law implies consent to emergency treatment."),
("A medical assistant tells other patients in the waiting room that a coworker is a drug user, which is untrue. Which tort is this?",
 "Slander", ["Libel", "Assault", "Abandonment"],
 "Slander is a spoken false statement that harms a person's reputation. If written or posted, it would be libel."),
("A false, damaging statement about a provider is posted in an online review. Which tort applies?",
 "Libel", ["Slander", "Battery", "Fraud"],
 "Libel is defamation in written or published form, including online posts. Spoken defamation is slander."),
("A physician stops seeing a patient in the middle of treatment without notice or a referral. Which tort could the patient claim?",
 "Abandonment", ["Assault", "Negligent hiring", "Breach of privacy"],
 "Ending a provider-patient relationship without reasonable notice or help finding other care is abandonment."),
("A medical assistant threatens to restrain a patient who refuses to sit still, and the patient becomes afraid. No one touches him. Which tort is this?",
 "Assault", ["Battery", "Slander", "Abandonment"],
 "Assault is a threat or act that causes a person to fear harmful contact. Battery requires actual unconsented touching."),
("The office receives a subpoena duces tecum. What does it order?",
 "Records to be produced", ["A witness to give testimony", "A patient to pay a bill", "A provider to stop practice"],
 "A subpoena duces tecum orders records or documents to be brought to court or a deposition. A plain subpoena orders a person to appear."),
("A provider gives sworn testimony in a lawyer's office before trial. What is this called?",
 "A deposition", ["An arbitration", "A subpoena", "A summons"],
 "A deposition is out-of-court testimony under oath, recorded for later use. Arbitration settles a dispute without a trial."),
("A medical assistant stops at a car crash and gives first aid within her training. What protects her if the injured person sues?",
 "Good Samaritan laws", ["Respondeat superior", "Res ipsa loquitur", "The statute of frauds"],
 "Good Samaritan laws protect people who give reasonable emergency help in good faith, without pay, outside their workplace. Details vary by state."),
("A physician decides to end care of a patient who repeatedly ignores treatment. How should the patient be notified?",
 "Certified letter, return receipt", ["By a voicemail or a text message", "By telling the front desk staff", "By a note left in the chart"],
 "A written letter sent certified with return receipt documents the notice. It gives a date care will end and offers help with records and referrals, and a copy goes in the chart."),
("Under the Controlled Substances Act, what describes a Schedule I drug?",
 "No accepted medical use", ["Lowest potential for abuse", "Sold over the counter", "Used only in hospitals"],
 "Schedule I drugs, such as heroin, have high abuse potential and no currently accepted medical use. Schedule V drugs have the lowest abuse potential."),
("Which schedule contains drugs with the lowest potential for abuse, such as some cough syrups with small amounts of codeine?",
 "Schedule V", ["Schedule I", "Schedule II", "Schedule III"],
 "Schedules run from I (highest abuse potential, no accepted use) to V (lowest). Low-dose codeine cough preparations are Schedule V."),
("Which events are reported to the state as vital statistics?",
 "Births and deaths", ["Office visits and refills", "Lab results and X-rays", "Insurance claims and payments"],
 "Vital statistics are births, deaths, fetal deaths, marriages and divorces, recorded by the state. Physicians sign certificates for births and deaths they attend."),
("A patient tests positive for a disease on the state's reportable list. To whom is it reported?",
 "The public health department", ["The patient's employer or school", "The patient's insurer", "The local newspaper"],
 "Reportable communicable diseases go to the local or state health department, which tracks outbreaks. HIPAA allows these public health disclosures."),
("What does the Patient Self-Determination Act require of facilities that take Medicare and Medicaid?",
 "Tell patients of directive rights", ["Write a living will for each patient", "Refuse care without a directive", "Keep directives from family"],
 "The PSDA requires hospitals, nursing homes, home health agencies and similar facilities to tell adult patients about their right to make advance directives and to ask whether they have one."),
("What does a living will do?",
 "States end-of-life care wishes", ["Names an heir for property", "Assigns an insurance payer", "Lists the patient's drug allergies"],
 "A living will states which life-sustaining treatments a person wants or refuses if they cannot speak for themselves, such as CPR or intubation."),
("Why does an EHR keep an activity log (audit trail)?",
 "To show who opened or edited it", ["To speed up claim payment", "To store lab images", "To remind patients of their visits"],
 "An audit trail records every access and edit with user, date and time, which deters snooping and supports HIPAA security reviews."),
]
